6|0@philadelphia^1439912910#cu74vk2:sensible_human$:&gt; I never knew you could drop them off at any station. That's what makes bike share bike share: one-way trips. That allows you to use it for transportation. If you returned it to the same station, that would be bike rental, not bike share. It would not be useful for transportation.
-2|1@philadelphia^1439914880#cu766jn:BroadStreet_Bully5$:What? You're still renting it.
2|1@philadelphia^1439915497#cu76lsx:sensible_human$:The difference is in the purpose and the function. It's exactly the same as the difference between car share and car rental. Bike share has existed for decades in Europe as a separate industry from bike rental. It began to catch on in the States after Capital Bikeshare was launched in DC in 2010. Bike rental (like car rental) is intended for a longer time period. This can be for recreational purposes or for transportation needs over an extended period of time. If you are returning it to the same place, it's not really useful as an every day transportation amenity. It is an agreement to temporary ownership, which is the exact opposite of sharing. Bike share is not rental because you are only using it while you are riding it and you are returning it to a different station where it is immediately available for others to use. That makes it part of the public transportation system. You can use it to connect to a bus or train station or to get to work, for example. Think of bike share as more like a public amenity and a public transportation system, while bike rental is more like a commercial business.
-4|1@philadelphia^1439932502#cu7i9qm:BroadStreet_Bully5$:It's not that deep.
5|0@philadelphia^1439936959#cu7l1pe:sensible_human$:Um, bike share is a big deal. It's a major change to urban transportation systems and has long term effects on transportation and land use planning. It's a pretty huge topic in the field of urban planning and will have a significant effect on how cities grow and evolve, as well as how bike infrastructure will develop and related transportation policy will change.
